##DESCRIPTION
##  Use derivatives to determine the multiplicity of a root.
##ENDDESCRIPTION

##KEYWORDS('multiplicity', 'polynomial')

## DBsubject('Calculus - single variable')
## DBchapter('Applications of Differentiation')
## DBsection('Misc.')
## Date('2/3/2018')
## Author('Peter Staab')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',         'PGML.pl',
    'PGpolynomialmacros.pl', 'contextFraction.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context('Fraction');
$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;
$showPartialCredit         = 0;

do {
    $a = non_zero_random(-5, 5);
    $b = non_zero_random(-3, 3);
} until ($a != $b);

@p  = (1);
$p1 = [ 1, -$a ];
$p2 = [ 1, -$b ];

$n1 = random(2, 4);

for ($i = 0; $i < $n1; $i++) {
    @p = PolyMult($p1, ~~@p);
}
$m1 = 5 - $n1;
for ($i = 0; $i < $m1; $i++) {
    @p = PolyMult($p2, ~~@p);
}

$poly1 = Compute(PolyString(~~@p))->reduce;

### e^{kx} - taylor

$n2 = random(2, 4);
$k2 = non_zero_random(-3, 3);

@tay2 = (1);
$fact = 1;
for ($i = 1; $i < $n2; $i++) {
    $fact *= $i;
    $coeff = Fraction($k2**$i, $fact)->reduce;
    push(@tay2, $coeff);
}

@tay2a = reverse @tay2;
$tay_f = Compute(PolyString(~~@tay2a))->reduce;
$arg2  = Compute("$k2 x")->reduce;
$g     = Compute("$tay_f-e^($arg2)");

### sin(kx) - taylor

$n3 = random(2, 3);
$k3 = random(1, 4);

@tay3    = (Fraction(-$k3**3, 6), 0, $k3, 0);
$tayout3 = PolyString(~~@tay3);
$arg3    = Compute("$k3 x")->reduce;
$h       = Compute("$tayout3-sin([$arg3])");

BEGIN_PGML

For each of the following functions and points, determine the multiplicity.

1) [`` f(x) = [$poly1] ``] at [` x= [$a] `]

	Multiplicity is [_______]{$n1}

2) [`` g(x) = [$g] ``] at [`x=0`]

	Multiplicity is [_______]{$n2}

3) [`` h(x) = [$tayout3] -\sin([$arg3]) ``] at [`x=0`]

	Multiplicity is [_______]{5}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_HINT
Recall that a function [`f(x)`] has a root of multiplicity [`k`] at [`x=x_0`] if
[`f^{(n)}(x_0) = 0 `] for [`n=0,1,2,\ldots,k-1`] and [`f^{(k)}(x_0) \neq 0`].

Therefore, take successive derivatives of the functions above, evaluate them at the
given point and determine when the deriative at the point is not zero.
END_PGML_HINT

$df = $poly1;
@fx0;
for (0 .. $n1 + 1) {
    $fx0[$_] = $df->eval(x => $a);
    $df = $df->D('x');
}

$dfs = "f($a)=";
$dfs .= "f^{($_)}($a)= " for (1 .. ($n1 - 1));

$dg = $g;
@gx0;
for (0 .. $n2 + 1) {
    $gx0[$_] = $dg->eval(x => 0);
    $dg = $dg->D('x');
}

$dgs = "g(0)=";
$dgs .= "g^{($_)}(0)=" for (1 .. ($n2 - 1));

$dh = $h;
@hx0;
for (0 .. 6) {
    $hx0[$_] = $dh->eval(x => 0);
    $dh = $dh->D('x');
}

$dhs = "h(0)=";
$dhs .= "h^{($_)}(0)=" for (1 .. 4);

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

For each of these, we'll use the fact that a function [`f(x)`] has a root of
multiplicity [`k`] at [`x=x_0`] if [`f^{(n)}(x_0) = 0 `] for [`n=0,1,2,\ldots,k-1`]
and [`f^{(k)}(x_0) \neq 0`].

1) For this function: [` [$dfs]0 `]  and [`f^{([$n1])}([$a])=[$fx0[$n1]] `],
so the multiplicity is [$n1]

2) For this function: [` [$dgs]0 `] and [`g^{([$n2])}(0)=[$gx0[$n2]] `],
so the multiplicity is [$n2]

3) For this function: [`[$dhs]0`]  and [`h^{(5)}(0)=[$hx0[5]] `],
so the multiplicity is 5.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
